  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1136 PM CDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nThe synoptic pattern aloft continues to be dominated by a very large \nhigh pressure complex over the southern CONUS and residing overhead \nof the forecast area. The only other aspect of the patttern \ncurrently is a very strong low pressure system that continues to \ndeepen over the Pacific Northwest. Today's convection greatly \nover preformed and has dropped temperatures down into the 70/80s. \nSome weak convection continues in very far SW Kansas, but CAMs have \nthese storms slowly dying off. Additionally, some CAM's have \nscattered overnight convection across the CWA that will all remain \nsub-severe. The best chances appear to be up near Hays in the \nnorthern counties at around 8 AM to noon. \n\nA  heat advisory remains in effect east of US 283 until Sunday at 8 \nPM. Highs Sunday are forecast in the mid-to-upper 90s and even \nexceeding triple digits in the far southeastern portion of the CWA. \nOther than the heat that continues to blanket the area, the forecast \nfocus is on more storm chances in the evening. CAMs have weak storms \ndeveloping in eastern Colorado around 7 PM and moving into Kansas. \nProgged CAPE and shear are very minimal and storms are expected to \nremain well below severe thresholds if the storms even materialize. \n\nMonday and Tuesday are forecast to continue the heat with highs in \nthe upper 90s. The next best chance for precipitation comes Tuesday \nnight with 30-40% storm chances via ensembles in the northwestern \ncounties. Although it is still early, it is expected that these \nstorms continue to be sub-severe with strong winds up to 50 mph \nbeing possible. Wednesday, ensembles have another wave of \nprecipitation, this time more widespread. More time will be needed \nbefore making declarative statements on the convection. The rest of \nthe forecast period will remain hot, with sunday even cooling down \nenough for highs be around 90 degrees. This would easily be the \ncoolest day of the forecast period. Through this stretch, modest \nrelative humidity minimums remain above 25% quelling most of the \nfire weather risk. This is forecast to accompany a continuing south \nwind at 10-15 mph.",
